completion_raw:
To terminate a C program, you can use the `return` statement with no argument to exit the main function immediately. This will cause the program to stop executing and return control to the operating system.

Here's an example:

```c
int main() {
    // Your program code here

    return 0; // Exit the program with a status of 0 (success)
}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `exit()` function from the `stdlib.h` library to terminate the program. This function takes an integer argument that specifies the exit status of the program.

Here's an example:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your program code here

    exit(0); // Exit the program with a status of 0 (success)
}
```

Both of these methods will immediately terminate the program and free any resources that have been allocated.

